# Infrastructure Economist Brief — Quiet-by-Design Standards at MWAA

**Run:** `quiet-by-design-standards-for-mwaa-terminals-2`
**Author:** Infrastructure Economist (independent Stage 1 research)
**Date:** 2026-08-07

## Framing

The Council thesis asks whether MWAA should adopt a common quiet-by-design terminal standard for IAD and DCA. Read as a capital-economics question, the interesting variable is not "quiet or loud." It is *when* the acoustic, PA-zoning, FIDS, and information-system requirements are written into the drawings — before the design freezes, or after the concrete has cured. Every dollar of that decision is measurable, and the difference between the two paths is one to two orders of magnitude at the point of construction. That is the economist's argument, and it is largely independent of whether the reader loves silent airports or loathes them. *(professional judgment)*

MWAA is at an unusually rich decision window for this. The 2025 Use & Lease has locked in an approximately $9 billion Capital Construction Program with airline-funded debt service [Source: https://www.mwaa.com/news/metropolitan-washington-airports-authority-completes-negotiations-new-use-and-lease-agreement]. The July 2026 announcement layers a $20–22.5 billion multi-decade Dulles transformation on top of that base [Source: https://viewfromthewing.com/dulles-airport-could-finally-kill-its-mobile-lounges-but-the-rebuild-plan-now-costs-22-billion/]. The airline cost per enplanement at IAD is reported at $12.77 today and MWAA's 2025A Official Statement forecasts it rising to $20.71 by 2030 — a 62% increase before the incremental transformation vision is priced in [Source: https://www.bondbuyer.com/news/dulles-expansion-raising-questions]. Every requirement that enters the Design Manual now travels through billions of dollars of concrete. Every requirement extracted through change order or post-BOD retrofit is paid for later at a punitive multiplier and, in a residual airline agreement, is paid for by the carriers who will price it back into fares. *(professional judgment on the economic mechanism)*

That is the frame. What follows are load-bearing findings, the evidence behind them, and the caveats.

## Key findings

1. **Acoustic and PA specification is a design-manual problem, not a construction problem.** The 1:10:100 heuristic — familiar to any capital cost engineer — holds that a defect corrected in design costs roughly $1, in construction $10, and in warranty/operations $100. Airport terminal PA and acoustics behave the same way, and worse when they collide with life-safety systems [Source: https://tradesunlimitedusa.com/2026/05/06/preventing-construction-rework-costs-1-10-100-rule/]. Under CM-at-Risk delivery, GMP lock occurs anywhere from 50–60% to 90–100% design; anything not in the drawings at that milestone reverts to change-order pricing [Source: https://airportscouncil.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/06/Airport-Owners-Guide-to-PDS-3rd-Edition-17June22.pdf].

2. **The reference case for "specify late" is Berlin Brandenburg, and its single largest failure was a public-address-adjacent life-safety system.** BER's initial 2006 budget of approximately €2 billion ended at roughly €7.3 billion at 2020 opening — a 2.6–3.6x overrun and a nine-year schedule slip [Source: https://budgetoverrun.com/case-studies/berlin-brandenburg-airport] [Source: https://www.airportwatch.org.uk/2020/10/nine-years-late-and-x3-over-budget-due-to-problems-berlins-brandenburg-airport-finally-opens-during-a-pandemic/]. The single biggest driver of the 2012 cancellation was a bespoke smoke-extraction and voice-notification integration that never passed certification against a design already frozen [Source: https://execution-atlas.com/en/articles/berlin-brandenburg-airport/]. That is the exact class of failure a quiet-by-design standard is meant to prevent, because voice evacuation, PA zoning, adjacent-zone spillover, and acoustic performance are governed by the same integrated audio-and-notification specification.

3. **U.S. airport capital demand is running well ahead of federal funding, which raises the marginal cost of every avoidable change order.** The FAA's 2025–2029 NPIAS identifies approximately $67.5 billion in AIP/BIL-eligible needs across roughly 18,100 projects, up about 8% from the prior cycle [Source: https://www.faa.gov/sites/faa.gov/files/airports/planning_capacity/npias/current/ARP-NPIAS-2025-2029-Narrative.pdf]. The broader ACI-NA 2025 Infrastructure Needs Study puts total five-year U.S. commercial-service airport need at $173.9 billion, with terminal buildings the largest single category at roughly 42% [Source: https://airportscouncil.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/03/2025-Infrastructure-Needs-Study.pdf]. Because AIP/PFC/BIL do not fully cover terminal work, incremental late-spec costs at MWAA flow disproportionately into airport-issued debt and airline rates, not federal grants. *(judgment on funding-stack incidence)*

4. **MWAA is already at the upper end of its own affordability curve, which sharpens the opportunity cost.** MWAA's 2025A Official Statement forecasts cost per enplanement rising from $12.77 to $20.71 by 2030 under the existing CCP; industry observers quoted in the trade press have postulated CPE trajectories up to "$90" if the $22.5B vision is executed at posted scope without offsetting revenue [Source: https://www.bondbuyer.com/news/dulles-expansion-raising-questions]. For reference, industry-cited CPE at peer large hubs runs $33.85 at LAX, $29.84 at ORD, and $4.48 at ATL [Source: https://www.bondbuyer.com/news/dulles-expansion-raising-questions]. In that environment, an avoidable eight- or nine-figure change order to retrofit PA zoning or ceiling absorption into a new concourse is not a rounding error — it is a measurable move in the airline rate base and, ultimately, in fares out of both airports. *(judgment)*

5. **The peer set proves "quiet-by-design" is a specification, not a policy.** London City Airport has operated a silent-terminal policy since 2008 [Source: https://www.londoncityairport.com/at-the-airport/need-to-know/airport-policies/silent-policy]; Helsinki since 2015 [Source: https://airport.nridigital.com/air_mar18/shhhh_is_the_day_of_the_airport_announcer_over]; Schiphol has run "virtually no announcements" for roughly a decade [Source: https://news.schiphol.com/silence-at-amsterdam-airport-schiphol/]. All three treat "silent" as an operating rule, but the terminals already meet acoustic and PA-intelligibility conditions that make silence viable. The U.S. failure mode is the reverse: airports market "quiet" while operating in echoic halls with legacy PA that require *more* announcements to overcome low speech intelligibility. That is a design-manual failure, not a governance failure. *(judgment on the pattern)*

6. **JFK Terminal 1 is the current U.S. reference case for the "specify it into the RFP" model at scale.** The $9.5 billion Terminal 1 is the anchor of a $19 billion JFK overhaul and is tracking to a mid-2026 opening, procured with integrated technology and passenger-experience specifications embedded in the P3 documents at award [Source: https://www.cnbc.com/2025/08/02/jfk-airport-new-international-terminal.html]. This is the delivery-model analog for the passenger-experience layer inside any new IAD concourse.

7. **The primary technical reference (ACRP Research Report 175) already exists and treats PA intelligibility as a design-integrated problem.** ACRP 175, *Improving Intelligibility of Airport Terminal Public Address Systems* (2017), provides guidelines addressing architectural design, PA system design, commissioning, announcements, and operations as a single integrated problem — precisely because retrofitting intelligibility into a hard-surface terminal is expensive and often technically impossible [Source: https://crp.trb.org/acrpwebresource2/improving-intelligibility-of-airport-terminal-public-address-systems/]. MWAA does not need to write from a blank sheet.

8. **The Denver baggage-system precedent is the operational warning to tape to the front of every design meeting.** DIA's automated baggage-system contract escalated from an initial $193 million to over $400 million, delayed opening by 16 months at roughly $1 million per day in carrying cost, and was ultimately abandoned in August 2005 [Source: https://peimpact.com/the-denver-international-airport-automated-baggage-handling-system/] [Source: https://sebokwiki.org/wiki/Denver_Airport_Baggage_Handling_System]. The failure was not the technology; it was integrating an ambitious system into a terminal whose design had frozen around a different assumption. Acoustics and PA are less glamorous but structurally identical: their performance depends on ceiling height, surface reflectivity, mechanical noise, and loudspeaker placement — all of which are locked in early. *(judgment on the structural analogy)*

## Evidence

### The MWAA capital and credit picture

- **~$9B CCP under a 15-year Use & Lease.** MWAA's 2024 press release confirms a new 15-year Airline Use & Lease taking effect January 1, 2025, alongside an approximately $9 billion capital construction program [Source: https://www.mwaa.com/news/metropolitan-washington-airports-authority-completes-negotiations-new-use-and-lease-agreement]. Trade-press reporting attributes the split as roughly $6.99B at IAD and $2.39B at DCA. The residual-cost structure and 140% coverage target frame airline-rate exposure to every marginal capital dollar.
- **$714.4M Series 2025A.** MWAA issued $714.4M of airport system revenue and refunding bonds in Series 2025A [Source: https://www.bondbuyer.com/news/mwaa-coming-to-market-with-829-million-municipal-bond-deal]. The 2025A Official Statement carries the CPE forecasts referenced above and the debt-issuance program.
- **Approximately $5.5B in additional debt through 2028** carrying adjusted debt per O&D enplanement toward roughly $400 by 2028, per the 2025A Official Statement (as summarized in the airport-context packet). MWAA's ratings — Moody's Aa3, S&P AA-, Fitch AA-, all stable at May 2025 affirmations — sit inside investment grade but not with headroom to absorb material change-order surprises. *(judgment on rating headroom)*
- **$20–22.5B Dulles transformation announced July 2026.** Joint MWAA/United/USDOT announcement of a decade-long rebuild of Concourses C/D, AeroTrain extension, mobile-lounge retirement, and more than 5 million sq ft of new/renovated space [Source: https://viewfromthewing.com/dulles-airport-could-finally-kill-its-mobile-lounges-but-the-rebuild-plan-now-costs-22-billion/] [Source: https://constructionreviewonline.com/washington-dulles-international-airport-slated-for-22bn-overhaul-in-proposed-revitalization-plan/]. Trade-press analysis flags that the announcement selects and accelerates a direction, but is not a single construction contract, and does not publish a project-by-project budget, gate count, or construction schedule [Source: https://crankyflier.com/2026/08/04/the-plan-for-dulles-is-ambitious-and-ridiculous-and-it-wont-happen/].

### The cost of specifying late

The 1:10:100 heuristic is a general one, but in airport terminal work it maps directly onto three concrete cost mechanisms. *(judgment)* First, architectural change: acoustic ceilings, wall absorption, mechanical acoustic isolation, and loudspeaker placement all depend on structural coordination that is only cheap to change before the GMP lock — which under CM-at-Risk can occur as early as 50–60% design [Source: https://airportscouncil.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/06/Airport-Owners-Guide-to-PDS-3rd-Edition-17June22.pdf]. Second, life-safety integration: NFPA 72 voice-evacuation intelligibility requirements are performance-based, and a system that fails commissioning tests forces demolition of finishes. Third, airline concurrence: majority-in-interest triggers in residual agreements make late scope adds politically expensive, not just financially, because they require re-briefing signatory airlines that have already priced the base program into their CPE tolerance. AACE International's Recommended Practice 100R-19 formalizes the contract-change-management discipline that governs this space [Source: https://web.aacei.org/docs/default-source/toc/toc_100r-19.pdf].

The BER case is worth pausing on because it is the cleanest cautionary tale in the modern terminal literature. Published post-mortems converge on the finding that fire-protection and voice-notification integration failed against a design frozen around a different smoke-extraction concept; correcting the mismatch consumed years, and eventual costs settled at roughly €6.5–7.3B against a starting budget of €2–2.83B [Source: https://budgetoverrun.com/case-studies/berlin-brandenburg-airport] [Source: https://execution-atlas.com/en/articles/berlin-brandenburg-airport/]. Denver's baggage system tells the same story in different language: a technology aspiration collided with a frozen architectural envelope and never recovered [Source: https://peimpact.com/the-denver-international-airport-automated-baggage-handling-system/]. In both cases, an early performance specification — even a modest one — would have averted the entire failure sequence. *(judgment on the counterfactual)*

### The peer implementations

London City's silent-terminal policy (2008), Helsinki's (2015), and Schiphol's ("virtually no announcements" since roughly 2011) are useful precisely because they demonstrate that "silence" is a downstream operating rule that only works when the upstream conditions are engineered. All three retain announcements for gate changes, disruption, and emergencies, with heavy reliance on FIDS and mobile channels for routine information [Source: https://www.londoncityairport.com/at-the-airport/need-to-know/airport-policies/silent-policy] [Source: https://airport.nridigital.com/air_mar18/shhhh_is_the_day_of_the_airport_announcer_over]. The measurable inputs — reverberation time, ambient noise floor, PA zoning, adjacent-space spillover, digital signage redundancy — are what make the operating rule work. Practitioner references cite RT60 below approximately 3.5 seconds, STI above approximately 0.45, and NC-55 ambient limits as typical benchmarks for terminal spaces [Source: https://www.zintraacoustic.com/articles/airport-acoustic-design-how-quiet-zones-and-sound-optimised-terminals-improve-passenger-experience]; these numbers should be validated against ACRP 175 and ANSI/ASA standards before adoption. In the absence of those inputs, "silent airport" is a marketing claim that collapses on the first irregular-operations day. *(judgment)*

### The federal funding backdrop

FAA's NPIAS 2025–2029 identifies $67.5B in AIP/BIL-eligible needs, up ~$5.1B (8%) from the prior cycle [Source: https://alerts.aaae.org/latest/regulatory_093024] [Source: https://www.faa.gov/sites/faa.gov/files/airports/planning_capacity/npias/current/ARP-NPIAS-2025-2029-Narrative.pdf]. ACI-NA's parallel 2025 study identifies $173.9B in total commercial-airport need over the same window, with terminal buildings the largest single share at roughly 42% [Source: https://airportscouncil.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/03/2025-Infrastructure-Needs-Study.pdf]. The gap between federal-eligible and total need is the airport-financed share — and it is exactly where a design-phase acoustic/PA standard converts into avoided airline-rate pressure. *(judgment)*

### The opportunity-cost argument

If MWAA absorbs an avoidable retrofit late in the CCP, the capital is not free; under a residual agreement the airlines fund the debt service, which shows up as CPE. At IAD, the base-case forecast already carries CPE from $12.77 to $20.71 by 2030 [Source: https://www.bondbuyer.com/news/dulles-expansion-raising-questions]; each additional $100M of avoidable capital, financed over 30 years at 5%, is roughly $6.5M in annual debt service and therefore roughly $0.22 in CPE against IAD's ~29M passengers [Source: airport-context.md §6, citing MWAA CY2025 traffic]. That is not a fatal number, but it is a measurable one, and it is the number that airlines will notice first. *(judgment on the arithmetic and its political incidence)*

## Caveats and limitations

1. **The MWAA Design Manual's current acoustic content is not on the public record.** The airport-context packet flags this as an open gap: whether the current volumes already contain NC/RC targets, RT60 ranges, STI thresholds, or PA loudspeaker density requirements is not disclosed publicly. This brief assumes the packet's read that they do not, or that any existing requirements are not integrated across acoustics, PA, FIDS, and operations. If the manual is more mature than public evidence suggests, the marginal case for a new standard weakens but does not disappear.

2. **The 1:10:100 heuristic is a heuristic.** It captures a well-attested pattern but does not resolve to a precise multiplier for airport-terminal PA and acoustic work. Published, comparable per-square-foot cost data on acoustic treatment as a share of terminal shell cost is scarce, and vendor-supplied numbers should be treated skeptically. The direction of the effect is unambiguous; the magnitude at MWAA would require the authority's own cost history to pin down.

3. **The $22.5B Dulles vision is not a signed program.** Trade-press analysis has flagged that the July 2026 announcement identifies scope and cost order-of-magnitude but does not publish a project-by-project budget, gate count, or construction schedule [Source: https://crankyflier.com/2026/08/04/the-plan-for-dulles-is-ambitious-and-ridiculous-and-it-wont-happen/]. Airline concurrence for the incremental scope beyond the CCP is not publicly confirmed. The economist's argument for standards-first specification is therefore contingent on how much of the vision proceeds; even a partial buildout, however, generates enough new terminal square footage to justify the design-manual work. *(judgment)*

4. **DCA and IAD are not symmetric.** DCA's approximately $2.4B is weighted toward gate-area capacity, curb/roadway, and repair; IAD's approximately $6.99B (plus potential $13–15B incremental) is dominated by new concourse construction. The economic case for embedding a standard in the Design Manual is stronger and more time-sensitive at IAD, where the largest window of new build is opening now. At DCA, the standard applies to retrofit and tenant-fit-out contexts where the marginal cost of compliance is higher and the achievable acoustic performance in existing envelopes is bounded. *(judgment)*

5. **Peer comparisons should be handled carefully.** London City, Helsinki, Schiphol, and Copenhagen operate under different regulatory regimes (voice-alarm intelligibility, ADA/ACAA equivalents, tenant-broadcast rights), and their announcement policies cannot be lifted whole-cloth into a U.S. hub. The transferable content is the specification discipline, not the operating rule. *(judgment)*

6. **What I could not verify from primary sources within this research window:** ACRP 175's specific STI/RT60 numerical recommendations (the report was inaccessible in a machine-readable format during the research window); the airport-financed share of the ACI-NA total by category with the exact table reference; and the specific MII language in the 2025 Use & Lease that would govern airline concurrence on a design-manual acoustic/PA update. These are named as gaps rather than filled with inference.

