## Bottom line for the Council

The infrastructure-economics answer to the run thesis is unambiguous: MWAA should adopt an integrated quiet-by-design standard *now*, embed it in the Design Manual and consultant scopes for the next round of IAD concourse work, and treat it as a life-safety-adjacent capital specification rather than a passenger-experience preference. The cost of writing the standard in 2026 is trivial against the cost of extracting the same performance through change orders in 2028–2033. Every serious cautionary tale in the modern terminal literature — Berlin, Denver, and the smaller ones the trade press does not remember — is a variation on the same failure: an operator that specified late, on a design that had already frozen, against a contract that no longer bent. MWAA is unusually well-positioned to avoid that pattern, and unusually exposed to it if the CCP scope hardens before the standard is written. *(judgment)*
